# Portfolio Allocation Comparison

This notebook compares portfolio weights generated by:
- Mean–Variance optimization using historical returns
- Black–Litterman optimization using posterior returns


import pandas as pd

from src.data_loader import load_price_data, compute_returns
from src.covariance import shrinkage_covariance
from src.optimizer import mean_variance_optimizer
from src.equilibrium import implied_equilibrium_returns
from src.black_litterman import black_litterman_posterior

tickers = ["RELIANCE.NS", "TCS.NS", "INFY.NS", "HDFCBANK.NS", "ICICIBANK.NS"]
start_date = "2019-01-01"
end_date = "2024-12-31"

prices = load_price_data(tickers, start_date, end_date)
returns = compute_returns(prices)

cov_matrix = shrinkage_covariance(returns)

historical_returns = returns.mean() * 252
weights_mv = mean_variance_optimizer(historical_returns, cov_matrix)
weights_mv

n = len(tickers)
market_weights = pd.Series([1/n] * n, index=tickers)

pi = implied_equilibrium_returns(cov_matrix, market_weights)

P = [[0, 1, -1, 0, 0]]
Q = [[0.03]]
omega = [[0.0001]]

posterior_returns = black_litterman_posterior(
    cov_matrix, pi,
    pd.DataFrame(P),
    pd.DataFrame(Q),
    pd.DataFrame(omega)
)

weights_bl = mean_variance_optimizer(posterior_returns, cov_matrix)
weights_bl

pd.DataFrame({
    "Mean–Variance Weights": weights_mv,
    "Black–Litterman Weights": weights_bl
})

## Conclusion

Mean–Variance optimization produces unstable and concentrated portfolios
because it relies entirely on noisy historical returns.

Black–Litterman produces more stable and diversified allocations by anchoring
investor views to market-implied equilibrium returns.
